WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.633 --> 00:00:10.199
Hello, my name is Elizabeth Blackburn and I'm going to tell you about

00:00:10.200 --> 00:00:14.432
the discovery of telomeric DNA and of telomerase.

00:00:14.433 --> 00:00:19.899
When I first started working on this question,

00:00:19.900 --> 00:00:23.966
the ends of chromosomes, and these are chromosomes here,

00:00:23.967 --> 00:00:27.966
were known to be very important for protecting the genetic material.

00:00:27.967 --> 00:00:32.366
But nobody knew what was there.

00:00:32.367 --> 00:00:37.732
Now, it was known that every chromosome had a long DNA molecule

00:00:37.733 --> 00:00:39.832
that goes from one end of the chromosome to the other,

00:00:39.833 --> 00:00:43.899
and that long DNA, of course, carries all the genes,

00:00:43.900 --> 00:00:50.199
and every time that chromosome is replicated, the DNA has to be fully copied.

00:00:50.200 --> 00:00:58.032
But it was also known around the 1970's that the DNA replication machinery of the cell

00:00:58.033 --> 00:01:03.066
wasn't able to completely copy all the way out to the very ends of the chromosome.

00:01:03.067 --> 00:01:09.132
And so the prediction of what might happen every time a DNA replicated,

00:01:09.133 --> 00:01:11.232
in order for a cell to divide,

00:01:11.233 --> 00:01:13.799
would look something like what's depicted in this slide here,

00:01:13.800 --> 00:01:17.866
which would be that the chromosomal DNA end region

00:01:17.867 --> 00:01:23.532
would get shorter and shorter and shorter until eventually the cells would have such short chromosomes

00:01:23.533 --> 00:01:26.866
that the would be perhaps missing something from their ends,

00:01:26.867 --> 00:01:29.266
and then not able to divide anymore.

00:01:29.267 --> 00:01:32.399
And that was even predicted on these theoretical grounds

00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:35.866
to be something that was called senescence,

00:01:35.867 --> 00:01:38.766
which had been seen in cells growing in culture,

00:01:38.767 --> 00:01:42.866
that they, in certain cases, could only go through a certain number of divisions,

00:01:42.867 --> 00:01:46.499
and then they couldn't divide any further, but nobody knew why.

00:01:46.500 --> 00:01:51.299
And so, how to approach this problem?

00:01:51.300 --> 00:01:56.999
Well, I began it by taking advantage of a particular kind of organism,

00:01:57.000 --> 00:01:59.099
this is called Tetrahymena thermophila,

00:01:59.100 --> 00:02:01.899
and it's a single-celled, ciliated protozoan,

00:02:01.900 --> 00:02:04.932
and you can see in this scanning electron micrograph here

00:02:04.933 --> 00:02:08.799
all of the cilia all over the single cell.

00:02:08.800 --> 00:02:12.032
And the particular reason for this choice of organism

00:02:12.033 --> 00:02:17.566
was that these cells have within them some very short linear chromosomes,

00:02:17.567 --> 00:02:22.766
in high numbers, and so what was known before

00:02:22.767 --> 00:02:28.332
was that, well, nobody knew what was at the ends of the chromosomes in eukaryotes,

00:02:28.333 --> 00:02:31.132
cells with nuclei, such as Tetrahymena,

00:02:31.133 --> 00:02:38.799
certain very short, linear viruses that grow in bacteria, called bacteriophages,

00:02:38.800 --> 00:02:43.732
had had the ends of their DNA analyzed,

00:02:43.733 --> 00:02:48.032
and in this organism, Tetrahymena, the very short chromosomes were about the same kind of size,

00:02:48.033 --> 00:02:50.866
and so I thought, well, I would try and look and see

00:02:50.867 --> 00:02:53.899
what was at the ends of these chromosomes,

00:02:53.900 --> 00:03:01.232
these mini-chromosomes. Now, at this point, DNA sequencing methods had not been developed.

00:03:01.233 --> 00:03:05.099
And so, although this is hard for you to believe, probably, these days,

00:03:05.100 --> 00:03:11.432
but that was the case. And so what I had to do was to rely on a different kind of approach

00:03:11.433 --> 00:03:18.132
and that was  to really try to stitch together pieces of information about the ends of the DNA,

00:03:18.133 --> 00:03:23.799
that I could obtain by using labeling techniques, radio-labeling techniques,

00:03:23.800 --> 00:03:26.899
which would incorporate radio labels into the DNA,

00:03:26.900 --> 00:03:31.999
and analyzing by combinations of enzymes and chemicals

00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:39.899
that would cleave the DNA into little pieces that were dependent upon the particular

00:03:39.900 --> 00:03:42.766
building block bases of the DNA.

00:03:42.767 --> 00:03:47.699
And patch together the sequences, sort of stitch it together like a jigsaw puzzle.

00:03:47.700 --> 00:03:52.299
So, for example, I would cut the DNA up and see that there were,

00:03:52.300 --> 00:03:55.032
for example, little motifs in it, like this one here,

00:03:55.033 --> 00:04:02.199
where there was this motif of four cytosine residues in a row, CCCC, that's C4.

00:04:02.200 --> 00:04:06.199
And in fact, furthermore, I could tell from some of these analyses

00:04:06.200 --> 00:04:11.299
that there was a purine, that is an A or a G,

00:04:11.300 --> 00:04:14.899
and then CCCC, and then an A or a G.

00:04:14.900 --> 00:04:17.899
Because of the way I'd chemically cleaved this DNA up.

00:04:17.900 --> 00:04:22.666
And furthermore, by quantifying what was there, I could find that there were actually

00:04:22.667 --> 00:04:30.732
many, many repeats of this little motif, at the ends of these short mini-chromosomes,

00:04:30.733 --> 00:04:36.032
and so, putting it all together, I was able to conclude

00:04:36.033 --> 00:04:40.766
that these little tiny chromosomes ended in something that had really never been expected,

00:04:40.767 --> 00:04:47.366
and that was a sequence, that I've drawn the strand which is the complement of the CCCC strand,

00:04:47.367 --> 00:04:51.799
it's actually TTGGGG. And just repeated over and over and over and over again,

00:04:51.800 --> 00:04:56.866
perhaps 20 to 50 different repeats.

00:04:56.867 --> 00:05:00.166
Now, the question was how do these repeats get there?

00:05:00.167 --> 00:05:05.799
Now, at this point, there were a lot of possibilities that you could draw on paper.

00:05:05.800 --> 00:05:09.299
And often in science, what you do is you take an observation,

00:05:09.300 --> 00:05:12.232
and you put it into the existing body of information,

00:05:12.233 --> 00:05:18.699
and you say, well, how can I explain my new finding, given the context of all the information that is known.

00:05:18.700 --> 00:05:22.599
So, we know about DNA, we knew about it's replication machinery,

00:05:22.600 --> 00:05:27.399
we know how it recombined, and there were certain well-worked out rules by then

00:05:27.400 --> 00:05:35.332
for this. But these kinds of DNA sequences were not obeying those rules.

00:05:35.333 --> 00:05:38.532
And I'll tell you how they weren't obeying those rules in a moment,

00:05:38.533 --> 00:05:41.532
but the first thing I think that's interesting is that

00:05:41.533 --> 00:05:45.966
there comes a point when you're working in science, where you know a lot of things,

00:05:45.967 --> 00:05:49.066
you're trying to fit your results into well-established principles,

00:05:49.067 --> 00:05:53.166
and then it comes to a point where it just won't fit in that box anymore,

00:05:53.167 --> 00:05:54.799
you just can't push it into that box anymore,

00:05:54.800 --> 00:05:58.599
and you have to say, well, let's entertain some other possibilities.

00:05:58.600 --> 00:06:02.032
Now the kinds of observations, and I won't go through them,

00:06:02.033 --> 00:06:08.566
were that there were variable numbers of repeats on these small, linear chromosomes,

00:06:08.567 --> 00:06:11.566
sometimes there were, you know, 30 or so, sometimes there were 50, 70,

00:06:11.567 --> 00:06:13.032
you know, there was different numbers of repeats.

00:06:13.033 --> 00:06:19.066
Well, that was already odd, didn't look like the bacterial viruses that people were familiar with,

00:06:19.067 --> 00:06:21.999
and then, there were some other observations,

00:06:22.000 --> 00:06:27.599
one of which came from the extraordinary biology of these particular organisms,

00:06:27.600 --> 00:06:29.966
of which Tetrahymena thermophila is an example.

00:06:29.967 --> 00:06:36.199
The way it's got very small chromosomes is because it has a stage in its development,

00:06:36.200 --> 00:06:41.799
right after fertilization when it chops its chromosomes up into small pieces,

00:06:41.800 --> 00:06:48.299
and then, these telomeric TTG4 repeats would appear at the ends.

00:06:48.300 --> 00:06:49.666
How did they get there?

00:06:49.667 --> 00:06:54.166
This was not obeying rules that were known for DNA,

00:06:54.167 --> 00:07:00.032
DNA was supposed to be copied from DNA, or at least recombined with DNA that was very similar.

00:07:00.033 --> 00:07:04.032
You weren't supposed to have two new sequences suddenly joined together.

00:07:04.033 --> 00:07:06.332
And where did these repeats come from anyway?

00:07:06.333 --> 00:07:11.166
So, I decided to look in extracts of the Tetrahymena cells

00:07:11.167 --> 00:07:17.532
and see if one could detect if there was some enzymatic activity that might be able to

00:07:17.533 --> 00:07:21.166
add this sequence, TTGGGG, over and over again,

00:07:21.167 --> 00:07:32.632
to the ends of linear DNAs. I didn't know what kind of assay, really, to use at this point.

00:07:32.633 --> 00:07:35.499
But I did know there was a stage in the life of these organisms

00:07:35.500 --> 00:07:37.999
when the very short chromosomes got created,

00:07:38.000 --> 00:07:48.199
and in fact, they were created by chopping up the DNA, for reasons that are a whole different story,

00:07:48.200 --> 00:07:53.166
and then, the telomeric, as we called, repeat sequences,

00:07:53.167 --> 00:07:56.632
were being somehow put onto the ends of these DNAs,

00:07:56.633 --> 00:08:02.366
often in places where there were no such sequences there before.

00:08:02.367 --> 00:08:06.599
So, how did such a different sequence get joined onto another piece of DNA?

00:08:06.600 --> 00:08:10.866
That was what we were trying to think about, answering the question,

00:08:10.867 --> 00:08:16.632
how did these repeats get put on.

00:08:16.633 --> 00:08:20.432
And so, after a lot of trying different assay conditions,

00:08:20.433 --> 00:08:24.932
I finally found a mixture that was able to give me the first hint

00:08:24.933 --> 00:08:28.999
that something was happening, because I could see increasing amounts of this sequence

00:08:29.000 --> 00:08:33.099
being synthesized, apparently from nothing, in the test tube.

00:08:33.100 --> 00:08:38.566
And then at that point, I was joined by a new student in my lab, Carol Greider.

00:08:38.567 --> 00:08:45.366
And so the challenge now was to simplify down this reaction

00:08:45.367 --> 00:08:52.132
that we had going on in order to be able to see if there really was an enzymatic activity

00:08:52.133 --> 00:08:56.299
that truly was doing what these experiments had been hinting at.

00:08:56.300 --> 00:09:00.766
And so Carol was able to refine and strip down the assay

00:09:00.767 --> 00:09:05.366
to its bare essentials to get to this following point, depicted here.

00:09:05.367 --> 00:09:10.166
So, we would make a DNA oligonucleotide, which is this colored bar here,

00:09:10.167 --> 00:09:15.132
made up of the building blocks, you can see, that looked like the end of a chromosomal DNA.

00:09:15.133 --> 00:09:19.166
And then, by adding the extract of Tetrahymena cells,

00:09:19.167 --> 00:09:24.199
right at a stage when they were known to be making new telomeres,

00:09:24.200 --> 00:09:28.266
because the chromosomes at that point in their life cycle were being chopped up,

00:09:28.267 --> 00:09:30.132
and telomeres were being added.

00:09:30.133 --> 00:09:34.899
So, I reasoned that they might be enriched for any such enzymatic activity,

00:09:34.900 --> 00:09:36.099
if such a thing existed.

00:09:36.100 --> 00:09:40.132
So, we made extract from cells right at this stage,

00:09:40.133 --> 00:09:44.966
and found just by adding simple salts, and two simple building blocks of DNA,

00:09:44.967 --> 00:09:54.632
dGTP and dTTP, that in fact, repeats were added to the ends of these DNA chromosome-end mimics.

00:09:54.633 --> 00:10:02.466
So, this was very exciting for us, because this said, ah ha,

00:10:02.467 --> 00:10:05.966
this really would be a potential way of solving this problem

00:10:05.967 --> 00:10:12.266
of DNAs getting shorter and shorter because, now here's a way of making DNA get longer.

00:10:12.267 --> 00:10:16.432
So, we had an enzyme activity, which was working in the test tube,

00:10:16.433 --> 00:10:21.432
and adding nucleotides that corresponded to the telomeric DNA sequences,

00:10:21.433 --> 00:10:26.832
to the ends of chromosomes, and we had to name this enzyme,

00:10:26.833 --> 00:10:31.332
because we couldn't say Tetrahymena thermophila telomere terminal transferase too many times,

00:10:31.333 --> 00:10:35.999
and so, there was a bit of a kind of a discussion through the lab,

00:10:36.000 --> 00:10:39.199
and actually Claire Wyman in my lab came up with the name telomerase,

00:10:39.200 --> 00:10:42.199
which we thought kind of made sense, because here was a telomere,

00:10:42.200 --> 00:10:46.366
the "telomer", and then "ase", sounds like an enzyme,

00:10:46.367 --> 00:10:49.932
and we were thinking of polymerase, where, you know,

00:10:49.933 --> 00:10:51.899
a polymerase is something that makes a polymer,

00:10:51.900 --> 00:10:55.066
so, we said, well, here's telomerase, that makes a telomere.

00:10:55.067 --> 00:11:01.366
So, we were happy to add this new word, which eventually made its way into the dictionary.

00:11:01.367 --> 00:11:07.466
Now, just to give you an example of the kind of way these reactions looked,

00:11:07.467 --> 00:11:12.499
what this is depicting here is an autoradiogram,

00:11:12.500 --> 00:11:20.799
which is a fractionated mixture of the reaction products,

00:11:20.800 --> 00:11:23.799
which have been labeled with trace amounts of radioactivity,

00:11:23.800 --> 00:11:27.266
and then they've been fractionated in electrophoresis,

00:11:27.267 --> 00:11:29.066
in what's called a DNA sequencing gel,

00:11:29.067 --> 00:11:35.632
and they start with just the very short DNA oligonucleotide,

00:11:35.633 --> 00:11:39.866
we call it a primer, and then the DNAs get longer and longer and longer,

00:11:39.867 --> 00:11:43.132
and you can see that there's this lovely repeating pattern,

00:11:43.133 --> 00:11:45.166
and that pattern is the 6-base repeats

00:11:45.167 --> 00:11:49.199
of the TTGGGG motif being repeated over and over.

00:11:49.200 --> 00:11:55.099
And you can see that with time, more and more of this gets added to the ends of chromosomes.

00:11:55.100 --> 00:11:59.699
So, this was a very visual kind of demonstration of this activity

00:11:59.700 --> 00:12:03.999
and we could quantify it and do many experiments to try and understand its nature.

00:12:04.000 --> 00:12:09.932
One of the things we found out, not too long after first finding this activity,

00:12:09.933 --> 00:12:16.399
was that the enzyme actually had within it an essential ribonucleic acid component.

00:12:16.400 --> 00:12:23.799
And what this does is depicted here. So, it had a ribonucleic acid component in it

00:12:23.800 --> 00:12:27.866
which made the enzyme very sensitive to the enzyme ribonuclease.

00:12:27.867 --> 00:12:30.799
Not what you'd expect if it were just a protein enzyme.

00:12:30.800 --> 00:12:35.332
And within this RNA, there was a sequence, shown in blue here,

00:12:35.333 --> 00:12:42.932
which was the exact complement of the TTGGGG sequence that we found

00:12:42.933 --> 00:12:44.932
was being synthesized in the test tube.

00:12:44.933 --> 00:12:51.299
So, with such a very strong hint, we decided this really was the enzyme

00:12:51.300 --> 00:13:01.699
and now the challenge was to find out if this really was behaving in the cells

00:13:01.700 --> 00:13:06.766
as it was behaving in the test tube, because what we were finding in the test tube

00:13:06.767 --> 00:13:12.066
was that a DNA, such as a mimic of a chromosomal end, shown in black here,

00:13:12.067 --> 00:13:17.466
could be elongated by addition of nucleotides and copying the template,

00:13:17.467 --> 00:13:21.966
this was our model, and that would now make the chromosomal end longer.

00:13:21.967 --> 00:13:25.166
Was that really happening in cells?

00:13:25.167 --> 00:13:32.866
So, the answer to this gave us a bonus answer as well.

00:13:32.867 --> 00:13:38.666
We were answering this question by making small changes in that blue sequence here,

00:13:38.667 --> 00:13:45.066
in the RNA, and then putting RNAs with the changed sequence into a cell,

00:13:45.067 --> 00:13:52.599
and asking if the changed sequence in the blue sequence caused a change in the DNA that was added.

00:13:52.600 --> 00:13:56.632
For example, if this templating mechanism, as it's called,

00:13:56.633 --> 00:14:01.399
were really true, then if we changed a particular C into a G,

00:14:01.400 --> 00:14:05.932
then instead of being copied by templated synthesis into a G,

00:14:05.933 --> 00:14:10.632
it would now be copied into a C, or for example, if we changed one of those A's into a G,

00:14:10.633 --> 00:14:15.799
it would not be copied into a C, instead of into its normal T.

00:14:15.800 --> 00:14:23.232
And in fact, that was what we were able to show in experiments that were done in cells

00:14:23.233 --> 00:14:26.632
by Guo-Liang Yu and John Bradley and Laura Attardi.

00:14:26.633 --> 00:14:34.766
Now that also gave us, very unexpectedly, another answer to this question.

00:14:34.767 --> 00:14:39.866
How do Tetrahymena cells respond when telomerase is not working?

00:14:39.867 --> 00:14:44.966
We weren't actually initially setting out to do this experiment,

00:14:44.967 --> 00:14:49.366
but serendipitously, one of those changes in the template sequence,

00:14:49.367 --> 00:14:51.599
or in fact a couple of different ones,

00:14:51.600 --> 00:14:54.699
gave the sorts of effects that are shown here.

00:14:54.700 --> 00:14:59.866
So, certain changes, for reasons to this day we really don't understand,

00:14:59.867 --> 00:15:04.099
would cause the enzyme, instead of simply synthesizing

00:15:04.100 --> 00:15:10.666
the corresponding repeated sequence with the corresponding mutation in the DNA

00:15:10.667 --> 00:15:16.232
that was copied from the template, instead these changes caused the enzymes to just choke up,

00:15:16.233 --> 00:15:19.566
and it wouldn't work, it just basically stopped the enzyme working,

00:15:19.567 --> 00:15:25.066
as though it just sort of got choked up in its active site and can't work properly.

00:15:25.067 --> 00:15:27.932
Now, that was very lucky for us because it allowed us to say,

00:15:27.933 --> 00:15:32.366
what happens when an enzyme that doesn't work is present in the cell?

00:15:32.367 --> 00:15:39.132
Now, what happened was that the telomeres started getting shorter and shorter,

00:15:39.133 --> 00:15:42.166
and then over the course of about 20-25 cell divisions,

00:15:42.167 --> 00:15:46.466
they progressively got shorter and shorter and then the cells ceased to divide,

00:15:46.467 --> 00:15:53.166
altogether. And so that told us something very important, which we can sort of

00:15:53.167 --> 00:15:57.766
summarize here. Tetrahymena cells are normally immortal;

00:15:57.767 --> 00:16:02.299
that is to say, they keep multiplying, pretty much forever.

00:16:02.300 --> 00:16:07.332
However, all we had to was to inactivate telomerase

00:16:07.333 --> 00:16:13.366
by this very small, surgical strike, in the central RNA component of the enzyme,

00:16:13.367 --> 00:16:19.699
which inactivated the enzyme, and now the cells became, if you will, mortal.

00:16:19.700 --> 00:16:21.966
They could have a certain number of cell divisions

00:16:21.967 --> 00:16:25.032
during which their telomeres progressively shortened,

00:16:25.033 --> 00:16:27.866
and got too short, and then the cells ceased to divide.

00:16:27.867 --> 00:16:35.966
So, loss of functional telomerase was leading to progressive loss of telomeric DNA

00:16:35.967 --> 00:16:38.599
from the chromosome end, just as had been predicted

00:16:38.600 --> 00:16:43.099
from the original predictions of DNA replication

00:16:43.100 --> 00:16:47.766
if there weren't some compensatory mechanism.

00:16:47.767 --> 00:16:56.666
And so, many cells have sufficient telomerase, and so they can now maintain telomeres at various lengths,

00:16:56.667 --> 00:17:00.699
but maintain them sufficiently well so cells can keep multiplying.

00:17:00.700 --> 00:17:05.432
We discovered telomerase really by trying to answer a very basic question

00:17:05.433 --> 00:17:12.632
which was how do chromosomal DNAs solve their problem of incomplete replication.

00:17:12.633 --> 00:17:20.232
And it was very much driven by first of all the idea that if there's some interesting problem in biology,

00:17:20.233 --> 00:17:24.232
you want to go for it and try and understand how nature is working,

00:17:24.233 --> 00:17:30.132
I think in the back of my head, I was always aware that nature tends to be very conserved

00:17:30.133 --> 00:17:33.632
in many of its most fundamental molecular mechanisms,

00:17:33.633 --> 00:17:37.232
as have been amply learned from the central dogma,

00:17:37.233 --> 00:17:42.066
of DNA information going to RNA going to protein,

00:17:42.067 --> 00:17:46.499
although interestingly, this was a case in which a perfectly normal cellular enzyme

00:17:46.500 --> 00:17:50.799
was breaking the rule, and RNA was being copied into DNA,

00:17:50.800 --> 00:17:57.499
something that people before had been thinking was only the kind of things that certain viruses,

00:17:57.500 --> 00:18:02.266
and retro-elements did, but they didn't realize until we found telomerase

00:18:02.267 --> 00:18:08.299
that in fact, copying RNA into DNA can be a normal part of a normal cell's life.

00:18:08.300 --> 00:18:14.966
So, we did think that this was probably fundamental and likely to be conserved

00:18:14.967 --> 00:18:18.632
throughout at least eukaryotes. I think what was unknown

00:18:18.633 --> 00:18:21.132
was really what the implications of this might be.

00:18:21.133 --> 00:18:26.632
And that has taken many years to work out and is still very much in the process

00:18:26.633 --> 00:18:31.999
of trying to be worked out, as it affects, for example, what happens to humans,

00:18:32.000 --> 00:18:37.199
because we seem to live our lives with a sort of transition, if you will,

00:18:37.200 --> 00:18:39.866
between situations in which telomeres are shortening,

00:18:39.867 --> 00:18:42.399
and situations in which telomeres are lengthening.

00:18:42.400 --> 00:18:48.732
And how that balance, and dynamics, are all played out over human life is something that's very interesting,

00:18:48.733 --> 00:18:53.766
and may well have implications for long term progression,

00:18:53.767 --> 00:18:57.566
for example, towards certain disease states.

00:18:57.567 --> 00:19:00.999
And so I think what I take home as a message from this

00:19:01.000 --> 00:19:07.632
is that one really wants to understand how biology works by working at it

00:19:07.633 --> 00:19:13.132
in the most sort of curiosity-driven, question driven ways.

00:19:13.133 --> 00:19:18.166
